        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">It is always moisture — the question is where.</strong> Milky haze inside a smooth film days after sealing is blushing. Cloudiness that comes and goes with the weather is vapour rising through the slab. Powdery white at joints and edges is efflorescence. White confined to low spots is a coat applied too thick.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Wait one dry week before acting, because mild blushing often clears itself. If it does not, and the sealer is solvent-based acrylic, a sparing xylene application reflows the film and releases the moisture in an afternoon. Water-based products do not re-dissolve and need stripping.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Never add another coat over the white. And if the plastic sheet test shows vapour moving up through the slab, stop trying to find a better film — switch to a breathable penetrating sealer that has nothing to trap.
          </p>
